{
  "$comment": "GENERATED by tools/build-style.mjs. Do not hand-edit.",
  "version": 8,
  "name": "canifishthis",
  "sources": {
    "atlas": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "pmtiles://atlas",
      "$comment": "our pipeline's tiles: geometry, identity and administrative geography. No regulation data — see pipeline/deliver/tiles/layers.py."
    },
    "imagery": {
      "type": "raster",
      "tiles": [
        "https://server.arcgisonline.com/ArcGIS/rest/services/World_Imagery/MapServer/tile/{z}/{y}/{x}?blankTile=false"
      ],
      "tileSize": 256,
      "maxzoom": 19,
      "external": true,
      "attribution": "Powered by <a href=\"https://www.esri.com\">Esri</a> · Source: Esri, Vantor, Earthstar Geographics, and the GIS User Community",
      "$comment": "SATELLITE IMAGERY — Esri World Imagery, ONLINE-ONLY (owner 2026-10-21, handoff/PLAN/app-integration/decisions.md; research and terms: handoff/PLAN/app-data/satellite/README.md). The URL is Esri's own ArcGIS Online World_Imagery MapServer tile endpoint, as the original app used it (2026-03); a build with an ArcGIS Location Platform key swaps it whole at run time (`imageryTiles`, EXPO_PUBLIC_ARCGIS_API_KEY) — never a key in this file. `blankTile=false`: where Esri has no imagery at a zoom (rural BC stops at z17-18) it answers 404 instead of a grey 'Map data not yet available' tile, and the renderer overzooms the parent. maxzoom 19 is Esri's (urban BC goes deeper); its tiles are 256 px, so the z18 camera asks for z19 tiles, and rural holes 404 to the parent. Never stored or packaged: Esri's item terms say the layer is 'not intended to be used to export tiles for offline'. The attribution is Esri's required form ('Powered by Esri' + the service's copyrightText, read 2026-10-21) and rides on the source so MapLibre's control shows it exactly while the layer is drawn; @app/core IMAGERY_CREDIT holds the same words for the app's credit list (tools/imagery.test.ts). Tiles send Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *, which WebGL needs."
    },
    "basins": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "pmtiles://atlas",
      "maxzoom": 7,
      "$comment": "THE SAME ARCHIVE AS `atlas`, read as a second source that STOPS at z7 — the last zoom the tile builder writes basin polygons at (`basin` maxzoom = BASIN_HANDOVER_Z - 1 in pipeline/deliver/tiles/layers.py). MapLibre overzooms a source past its maxzoom, so the z7 tiles carry the field across [7, 8) up to the handover (z8 since T130, owner 2026-10-22: \"should be able to zoom out quite a bit more first\"; it was z10). An archive built while the handover was z9 also holds z8 basin tiles; they are never asked for. On `atlas` itself the field could never outlive the tiles: at z9 MapLibre asks the archive for z9 tiles, which carry no basins, and the field vanished a zoom before the rivers came back. Only the basin layer reads it, and that layer is drawn only while the field is on (`field.drawnIn`), so the Map tab never fetches through it."
    },
    "mus": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "pmtiles://atlas",
      "maxzoom": 12,
      "$comment": "THE SAME ARCHIVE AS `atlas`, read as a source that STOPS at z12 for the management units (T202, owner 2026-10-27). The tile builder writes `mu` only to z12, in its own pass with z12 as that pass's top zoom (pipeline/deliver/tiles/tippe.py `MU_MAX_ZOOM`), so z12 keeps a finer edge (~8 m at most on the ground) and MapLibre overzooms it from z13 to the camera's z18. On `atlas` the units would vanish at z13, where the archive has none. Saves ~7 MB: in the atlas every z13 tile also holds streams, so mu's squares never deduped there. TO REVERSE: set `MU_MAX_ZOOM` back to the archive's max zoom in tippe.py, point the `mu` layer back at `atlas` and drop this source."
    }
  },
